# Rust

## Crate boundaries

`scripts/architecture/crate-edges.json` is the dependency policy: every workspace member and the path dependencies it may have, split into `normal` and `dev`. `make architecture-check` compares it with `cargo metadata`. Cargo prevents cycles; this check prevents an otherwise valid edge from crossing an ownership boundary. Don't add an edge to the policy just to silence a failure: say why ownership changed.

- `crates/core` (package `{{project_slug}}-core`) and other library crates hold the logic. No Tauri, testable without a webview.
- `src-tauri/` is a thin shell: commands, channels, windows, plugins. Anything not Tauri-specific moves into a crate.
- Library crates compile on every target platform. Platform code is gated with `#[cfg(target_os = ...)]` at module level, never sprinkled through function bodies; don't gate on `target_arch` when you mean the OS.
- Prefer crates that bundle or vendor their C libraries, so the app needs nothing installed on the user's machine.

## Errors

- Library crates define typed errors with `thiserror`; callers match on variants. Never `anyhow` in a library's public API.
- The shell and binaries use `anyhow` with `.context("what we were doing")` at each boundary.
- Commands return `Result<T, CommandError>` (serializable, specta-typed, tagged by `kind`).
- No `.unwrap()`, `.expect()`, `panic!`, or panicking indexing outside tests; the workspace lints deny `unwrap_used`, `expect_used`, and `panic`, and clippy's config allows them in tests. An invariant proven locally takes a scoped `#[expect(clippy::expect_used, reason = "why this cannot fail")]`.
- A background task (a ticker, a watcher) never takes the process down. It logs once, rate-limited, and carries on.
- `unsafe` lives in one module per API, with a `// SAFETY:` comment on every block.

## Lints and formatting

`cargo fmt --all` and `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`. Fix warnings; an `#[allow]` needs a comment saying why, and prefer `#[expect(..., reason = "...")]` so it fails once obsolete.

## Testing

- Unit tests in `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` next to the code; integration tests in `crates/<crate>/tests/`.
- Fake at traits (a clock or ticker, a data source), never internal modules. Use real storage (the actual database engine, temp files) where storage is involved.
- Timing fakes reproduce real timing: drive time explicitly with paused tokio time or a fake clock, including a skipped tick and a long gap. A fake that always ticks on time hides boundary bugs.
- Tests that need real hardware or a display are `#[ignore]` with a reason and run by hand.
- Scope while iterating (`cargo test -p <crate> <name>`), then the workspace once the change is done.
